; 10-use-exact-arithmetic-with-calc.kurt
;
; Use exact arithmetic with `calc`
;
; `calc on` enables exact computation for the symbols bound by `number.kurt`. Integers, finite
; decimals, and fractions are represented exactly; Kurt does not use binary floating-point
; approximations for proof steps.

load number
calc on

2 + 3 = 5
0.1 + 0.2 = 0.3
2 ^ 10 = 1024
7 / 2 = 3.5

; `calc` computes when all required values are known. It does not solve equations for unknowns.
; For example, matching `$b / 3` against `0` does not infer `$b = 0`; state or prove that
; algebraic step using theory rules.
;
; `calc` also does not unfold a function you defined yourself with recursive axioms, e.g. a
; factorial `fact` (lesson 06): only the small set of built-in, provably-terminating operations
; (`+`, `*`, ...) are folded. Your own axioms are accepted on faith, with no termination check, so
; automatically unfolding them until done could loop forever on a badly-stated recursion -- unlike
; `+`/`*`/..., there is no trusted, terminating procedure behind them. `fact(3) = 6` has to be
; proved one step at a time instead (doc/kurt-ref.md §11.1, "Not implemented, maybe never").
;
; Turn calculation off when you need an unreduced expression as a rewrite target:

;     calc off
;     use 8 = 2 ^ 3
